Keep a restarted HLS run from serving its segments half-written.

Jellyfin serves segment N once N+1 exists. When a client retries a remux
one segment back (on 2026-11-01 the Projector Roku started at 255, then
at 254), the abandoned run's 255.ts is still on disk, so the new run's
254.ts is served while ffmpeg is writing it and Kestrel throws "Response
Content-Length mismatch: too many bytes written". The segment muxer from
jellyfin-remux-segment-cuts.patch writes each file in place for about
0.5 s at -readrate 10, where the hls muxer buffers a segment and writes it
in one burst, so this went from once in October to six times that night.
A stop report's kill also deletes every file with the playlist's prefix
1.5 s later, the new run's included.

Before starting a run, delete this playlist's segments at and after its
first segment, and have the delayed delete skip files written after the
killed ffmpeg exited. Upstream 12.4 has the same readiness test and the
same delete.

